@end menu

@node Options for GNATstack
@section Options for GNATstack

@noindent
@value{gnatstack} is configurable with respect to the amount of
information that we want to obtain. The set of options available is
the following:

@table @option
@c !sort!
@item -a
@cindex @option{-a}
Consider all subprograms as entry points.

@item -ca
@cindex @option{-ca}
Extract all possible cycles in the call graph.

@item -c @var{n}
@cindex @option{-c}
Use @var{n} bytes frame for subprograms at the beginning of cycles. This can
be used to increase visibility of cycles in reports.

@item -g
@cindex @option{-g}
Generate internal debug information.

@item -aO @var{dir}
@cindex @option{-aO}
Load @file{.ci} files from @var{dir}.

@item -e @var{entry_point[,...]}
@cindex @option{-e}
Name of symbols to be used as entry points for the analysis.

@item -files=@var{file}
@cindex @option{-files}
Take as arguments the files listed in text file @var{file}.
Text file @var{file} may contain empty lines that are ignored.
Each non empty line should contain the name of an existing file.
Several such switches may be specified simultaneously.

@item -h or --help
@cindex @option{-h}
@cindex @option{--help}
Output a message explaining the usage of @command{gnatstack}.

@item -l @var{n}
@cindex @option{-l}
Print the @var{n} subprograms requiring the biggest local stack
usage. By default none will be displayed.

@item -o=@var{x}
@cindex @option{-o}
Specifies the order for displaying the different call graphs.
The options allowed for this qualifier are:

@table @option
@c !sort!

@item -o=s
@cindex @option{-o=s}
Select stack usage order (the default mode).

@item -o=a
@cindex @option{-o=a}
Select alphabetical order.

@end table

@item -p
@cindex @option{-p}
Print all the subprograms that make up the worst-case path for every
entry point (the default mode).

@item -q
@cindex @option{-q}
Be quiet: do not display progression messages.

@item -np
@cindex @option{-np}
Do not print worst-case path for entry points.

@item -f @var{file}
@cindex @option{-f}
Write the generated graph (VCG format) into @var{file}.

@item -r @var{regexp}
@cindex @option{-r}
Any symbol matching the regular expression @var{regexp} will be
considered as an entry point for the analysis.

@item -d @var{n}
@cindex @option{-d}
Set default stack size for unbounded (dynamic) frames to @var{n} bytes.

@item -u @var{n}
@cindex @option{-u}
Set default stack size for unknown (external) calls to @var{n} bytes.

@item -v
@cindex @option{-v}
Specify the amount of information to be displayed about the
different subprograms. In verbose mode the full location of the
subprogram will be part of the output, as well as detailed information
about inaccurate data.

@item --version
@cindex @option{--version}
Print version information.

@item -W@var{x}
@cindex @option{-W}
Warning mode. The options allowed for this qualifier are:

@table @option
@c !sort!

@item -Wa
@cindex @option{-Wa}
Turn on all optional warnings.

@item -Wc
@cindex @option{-Wc}
Turn on warnings for cycles (recursion).

@item -Wu
@cindex @option{-Wu}
Turn on warnings for unbounded frames.

@item -We
@cindex @option{-We}
Turn on warnings for external calls.

@item -Wi
@cindex @option{-Wi}
Turn on warnings for indirect (including dispatching) calls.

@end table

@item -x
@cindex @option{-x}
Generate file @var{stack_usage.xml} with the information in XML format.

@item -oc=@var{name}
@cindex @option{-oc}
Use the @var{objcopy} executable @var{name} to extract the @file{.ci}
files from the object files. If may contain the prefix (such as
@var{powerpc-elf-objcopy}) or the suffix (such as @var{objcopyppc}).

@item -s=@var{section}
@cindex @option{-s}
Extract the @file{.ci} files from section named @var{section} in the
object files.

@item -k
@cindex @option{-k}
Keep the temporary files created by GNATstack (such as the @file{.ci}
files extracted from the object files.

@end table

@node Use of Project Files
@section Use of Project Files

@noindent
@command{gnatstack} can be invoked through the @command{gnat} driver
(see @cite{GNAT User's Guide}) and hence take advantage of the support
for project files. The list of project-related switches that are
available are:

@table @option

@item -P@var{project}
@cindex @option{-P}
Indicates the name of a project file. This project file will be parsed with
the verbosity indicated by @option{-vP@emph{x}}, if any, and using the
external references indicated by @option{-X} switches, if any.
@ifclear vms
There may be zero, one or more spaces between @option{-P} and @var{project}.
@end ifclear

@noindent
There must be only one @option{-P} switch on the command line.

@noindent
Since the Project Manager parses the project file only after all the switches
on the command line are checked, the order of the switches @option{-P},
@option{-vP@emph{x}} or @option{-X} is not significant.

@item -X@var{name=value}
@cindex @option{-X}
Indicates that external variable @var{name} has the value @var{value}.
The Project Manager will use this value for occurrences of
@code{external(name)} when parsing the project file.

@ifclear vms
@noindent
If @var{name} or @var{value} includes a space, then @var{name=value}
should be put between quotes.
@smallexample
  -XOS=NT
  -X"user=John Doe"
@end smallexample
@end ifclear

@noindent
Several @option{-X} switches can be used simultaneously. If several
@option{-X} switches specify the same @var{name}, only the last one is
used.

@noindent
An external variable specified with a @option{-X} switch takes
precedence over the value of the same name in the environment.

@item -U
@cindex @option{-U}
When used with a project file and no @file{.ci} files on the command
line, the @file{.ci} files for all sources of all project files are
passed when invoking @command{gnatstack}.

@item -vP@emph{x}
@cindex @option{-vP}
Indicates the verbosity of the parsing of GNAT project files.

@ifclear vms
@option{-vP0} means Default;
@option{-vP1} means Medium;
@option{-vP2} means High.
@end ifclear

@ifset vms
There are three possible options for this qualifier: DEFAULT, MEDIUM and
HIGH.
@end ifset

@noindent
The default is @option{Default}: no output for syntactically correct
project files.
@noindent
If several @option{-vP@emph{x}} switches are present, only the last
one is used.
@end table

@noindent
There is an optional package in the project file for @value{gnatstack}
(which is the package @code{Stack}) that has an unique attribute
@code{Switches}. This attribute is a simple variable that contains a
string list value representing the switches to be passed to
@command{gnatstack}.

@smallexample @c projectfile
@group
project Prj is
   package Stack is
      for Switches use ("-p");
   end Stack;
end Prj;
@end group
@end smallexample

@noindent
The following command will parse the project file @file{prj.gpr} and
call @command{gnatstack} with the specified options.

@smallexample
$ gnat stack -P prj.gpr
@end smallexample

@noindent
When @command{gnat stack} is used with a project file, but with no source
specified on the command line, it passes to the @command{gnatstack}
tool the set of @file{.ci} files that correspond to the units that
belong to the project. When adding the @option{-U} option all projects
in the project tree will be taken into account.

@node Information in Non-Loadable Section
@section Information in Non-Loadable Section

@noindent
The @file{.ci} file generated for every compilation unit can be written
into the generated object as a non-loadable section. For example, it can
be written into a section named @var{.GNU.callgraph} doing:

@smallexample
$ gcc -c -fcallgraph-info=su main_unit.adb
$ objcopy --add-section .GNU.callgraph=main_unit.ci main_unit.o
$ gnatstack main_unit.o
@end smallexample

@noindent
This section is not downloaded into the target so it does not affect the
execution. The advantage is that it may facilitate the retrieval of the
required @file{.ci} files (we simply need the list of object files that
make up the application), and it may help guaranteeing the coherence
between the call-graph and stack usage information and the corresponding
object files.

The linker would merge (concatenate) the @file{.ci} sections corresponding
to the object files being linked into the final executable (including those
coming from library files).

@value{gnatstack} can later extract this section from the object files
and use the information for the rest of the analysis. @value{gnatstack}
uses the @code{objcopy} tool to extract the @file{.ci} files.

By default, @value{gnatstack} uses the native @code{objcopy} executable.
It can be changed using the @option{-oc} option (see @ref{Options for
GNATstack}).

@smallexample
$ powerpc-elf-gcc -c -fcallgraph-info=su main_unit.adb
$ powerpc-elf-objcopy --add-section .GNU.callgraph=main_unit.ci main_unit.o
$ gnatstack -oc=powerpc-elf-objcopy main_unit.o
@end smallexample

@noindent
The section where the @file{.ci} files are stored can also be changed, and
@value{gnatstack} provides an option (@option{-s}, see @ref{Options for
GNATstack}) to specify this section.

@smallexample
$ gcc -c -fcallgraph-info=su main_unit.adb
$ objcopy --add-section my_section=main_unit.ci main_unit.o
$ gnatstack -s=my_section main_unit.o
@end smallexample

@c *********************************
@node Additional Capabilities
@chapter Additional Capabilities

@noindent
As indicated in @ref{The GNATstack Tool}, there is a list of
potential problems when computing stack requirements: indirect and
external calls, unbounded frames, and recursion. This chapter provides
guidance about how to address these issues.

The first possibility to address these issues is to avoid the
occurrence of these events by means of using the appropriate
restrictions and coding style rules. The second is to supply
additional information that will help @value{gnatstack} to proceed with
the analysis.

@itemize @bullet
@item Indirect calls.
@cindex Indirect calls
@cindex Dispatching calls
Determining statically the target subprogram when dereferencing an
access-to-subprogram variable is not always possible. These
occurrences can be forbidden through @code{pragma Restrictions}. The
use of @code{No_Access_Subprograms} avoids access-to-subprogram
types. Dispatching calls can also be restricted by using
@code{No_Dispatch} (which prohibits classwide constructs), or the less
restricting @code{No_Dispatching_Calls} (which ensures that no
dispatching calls are present).
@cindex @code{pragma Restrictions}
@cindex @code{No_Access_Subprograms} restriction
@cindex @code{No_Dispatch} restriction
@cindex @code{No_Dispatching_Calls} restriction
The @option{-Wi} option can be used to signal all the occurrences of
indirect calls in the program under analysis.
@cindex @option{-Wi}

@item External calls.
@cindex External calls
These are calls to subprograms for which no stack usage or call graph
information is available, introducing an unknown amount of stack usage
in a call chain. It can only be avoided by providing the required
information for the objects and libraries that make up the
application.
The @option{-We} option can be used to signal all the occurrences of
external calls in the program under analysis.
@cindex @option{-We}

@item Unbounded frames.
@cindex Unbounded frames
The use of dynamically sized local objects (such as those whose size
depends on an input argument) makes it impossible to statically bound
stack usage.
The @option{-Wu} option can be used to signal all the occurrences of
unbounded frames in the program under analysis.
@cindex @option{-Wu}

@item Recursion.
@cindex Recursion
Very simple cases can be detected by using @code{pragma Restrictions
(No_Recursion)}. This restriction ensures that as part of the
execution of a subprogram the same subprogram is not invoked. More
complex situations require call graph analysis. @command{gnatcheck},
see @cite{GNAT User's Guide}, can be used to detect potential
recursion. @value{gnatstack} can also detect all the occurrences of
cycles in the call graph using the options @option{-ca -Wc}.
@cindex @code{pragma Restrictions}
@cindex @code{No_Recursion} restriction
@cindex @option{-ca}
@cindex @option{-Wc}

@end itemize

@noindent
If the occurrence of these potential problem cannot be avoided,
@value{gnatstack} provides the means to supply additional stack usage
and call graph information.

When @value{gnatstack} finds incomplete information, such as external
and indirect calls, it generates a file (@file{undefined.ciu})
containing placeholders for the missing information. The format of
this file is similar to the @file{.ci} files, but with @code{XXX}
where the user information must be inserted.

For example, entries generated by @value{gnatstack} for external calls
have the following pattern:
@smallexample
node: @{ title: "ext_proc" label: "Ext_Proc\n/path/file.ads:1:2\nXXX bytes" @}
@end smallexample

@noindent
User-defined values can be specified in a @file{.ci} file by simply
replacing the @code{XXX} by the estimated value.

For indirect calls, the @file{undefined.ciu} file contains entries
like:
@smallexample
edge: @{ sourcename: "source" targetname: "XXX" priority: 100 linestyle: solid @}
@end smallexample

@noindent
In this case, the list of potential targets for the indirect call can
be placed in a user-defined @file{.ci} file, replacing the @code{XXX}
by the symbol name of the potential target of the call:
@smallexample
edge: @{ sourcename: "source" targetname: "target1" priority: 100 linestyle: solid @}
edge: @{ sourcename: "source" targetname: "target2" priority: 100 linestyle: solid @}
edge: @{ sourcename: "source" targetname: "target3" priority: 100 linestyle: solid @}
@end smallexample

@noindent
When there is some missing information the result of the static stack
analysis is not accurate. This situation is indicated by
@value{gnatstack} when displaying the results with the following
warning message:

@smallexample
Worst case analysis is *not* accurate because of ...; use -Wa for details
@end smallexample

@noindent
In addition @value{gnatstack} provides accuracy information on a
per-subprogram basis. The mark @code{*} is attached to the
subprograms when there is any missing information.

The accumulated worst-case stack usage for the different entry points
will contain the mark @code{+?} to indicate that extra stack
utilization may happen.

@smallexample
$ gnatstack *.ci
[...]
Worst case analysis is *not* accurate because of cycles, external calls.
Use -Wa for details.

Accumulated stack usage information for entry points

main : total 504+? bytes
 +-> main
 +-> proc_a *
 +-> proc_b *
@end smallexample


@c *********************************
@node Examples
@chapter Examples

@noindent
This section contains a number of examples of the use of
@value{gnatstack} showing how to use the tool and how to interpret
the results. Let us start analyzing a simple program like the
following:

@smallexample @c ada
@group
procedure Main_Unit is
   type Data_Type is array (1 .. 5) of Integer;

   function Inverse (Input : Data_Type) return Data_Type is
      Result : Data_Type;
   begin
      for Index in Data_Type'Range loop
         Result (Index) := Input (Data_Type'Last -
                                  (Index - Data_Type'First));
      end loop;

      return Result;
   end Inverse;

   Data   : Data_Type := (1, 2, 3, 4, 5);
   Result : Data_Type;
begin
   Result := Inverse (Data);
end Main_Unit;
@end group
@end smallexample

@noindent
A typical project file would contain the flags to compile the
application with the required options to generate stack consumption
and call-graph information. In addition, it would contain the desired
option for the stack analysis phase:

@smallexample @c projectfile
@group
project Prj is
   for Main use ("main_unit.adb");

   package Compiler is
      for Default_Switches ("Ada") use ("-fcallgraph-info=su");
   end Compiler;

   package Stack is
      for Switches use ("-p");
   end Stack;
end Prj;
@end group
@end smallexample

@noindent
We can then easily compile and analyze the application using this
project file:

@smallexample
$ gnat make -P prj.gpr
[...]
$ gnat stack -P prj.gpr
Accumulated stack usage information for entry points

main : total 376 bytes
 +-> main
 +-> main_unit
 +-> main_unit.inverse
@end smallexample

@noindent
Note that we do not need to specify the @file{.ci} files for the
application. The @command{gnat} driver computes automatically this
list. In addition, the main entry point has also been automatically
detected by @value{gnatstack}.

The execution of the tool tells that the longest path (in terms of
stack consumption) is that made up by @code{main} (the program entry
point) -> @code{main_unit} (the main Ada program) ->
@code{main_unit.inverse}. The maximum stack
consumption following this path is 376 bytes, and this bound can be
trusted.

Focussing on the capability of detecting problems related to the
computation of stack requirements we can use the following example:

@smallexample @c ada
@group
package Ext is
   procedure Set_Alignment (Minimum : Integer);
   function Get_Size (Value : Integer) return Integer;
end Ext;

package body Ext is
   Threshold : Integer;

   procedure Set_Alignment (Minimum : Integer) is
   begin
      Threshold := Minimum;
   end Set_Alignment;

   function Get_Size (Value : Integer) return Integer is
   begin
      return (((Value - 1) / Threshold) + 1) * Threshold;
   end Get_Size;
end Ext;

with Ext;
--  Ext is compiled without stack usage information

procedure P is
   Set_Alignment : access procedure (Minimum : Integer) :=
     Ext.Set_Alignment'Access;
   --  Indirect call

   procedure R;

   procedure Q is
      Data : array (1 .. Ext.Get_Size (10)) of Character;
      --  Dynamically sized local objects
   begin
      R;
   end Q;

   procedure R is
   begin
      Q;
   end R;
begin
   --  Set minimum alignment (via indirect call)
   Set_Alignment.all (Integer'Alignment);

   --  Recursion: Q -> R -> Q
   Q;
end P;
@end group
@end smallexample

@noindent
To compile and analyze this program we do:

@smallexample
$ gcc -c ext.adb
$ gnatmake p.adb -cargs -fcallgraph-info=su,da
[...]
$ gnatstack *.ci
[...]
Worst case analysis is *not* accurate because of cycles, unbounded frames,
external calls, indirect calls. Use -Wa for details.

Accumulated stack usage information for entry points

main : total 472+? bytes
 +-> main
 +-> p
 +-> p.q *
 +-> p.r *
@end smallexample

@noindent
The result of the analysis is not accurate. As indicated in the warning
message, we can check which is the origin of this inaccuracy using the
@option{-W} option. Additional information can be extracted increasing
the verbosity level. Adding the @option{-v} option will also print
detailed information about the location of the different subprograms
and per-subprogram stack requirements:

@smallexample
$ gnatstack -Wa -v -p *.ci
[...]

List of cycles:

<c1> p.q
 +-> p.q at Q:p.adb:11:14 : 144+? bytes (unbounded,cycle) (<c1>)
 +-> p.r at R:p.adb:9:14 : 104+? bytes (cycle) (<c1>)
 +-> p.q at Q:p.adb:11:14 : 144+? bytes (unbounded,cycle) (<c1>)

List of subprograms with dynamic unbounded frames:

  In p.q at Q:p.adb:11:14 (not directly reachable)
    Data at p.adb:12

List of external subprograms:

  ext.get_size at Get_Size:ext.ads:3:13

List of indirect (including dispatching) calls:

  1 indirect calls in: p at P:p.adb:4:11
    at p.adb:24

Accumulated stack usage information for entry points

main : total 472+? bytes (unbounded,cycle)
 +-> main at main:b~p.ads:24:14 : 112 bytes
 +-> p at ada_main_program:b~p.adb:28:17,P:p.adb:4:11 : 112 bytes
 +-> p.q at Q:p.adb:11:14 : 144+? bytes (unbounded,cycle) (<c1>)
 +-> p.r at R:p.adb:9:14 : 104+? bytes (cycle) (<c1>)
@end smallexample

@noindent
We can see that the result is not accurate because there is recursion,
dynamic frames, and symbols for which there is no stack
usage information.

@value{gnatstack} has detected one cycle in the call graph (@code{Q}
calling @code{R} which in turn calls @code{Q}). The cycle has been
tagged as @code{<c1>} for later references. The following section of the
report indicates that the use of a dynamically sized local object
(@code{Data}) derives into a dynamic unbounded frame (subprogram
@code{Q}). We then have the list of subprograms for which there is no
stack usage information (package @code{Ext} has been compiled without
the required flags). Finally, there is a list of locations where there
is an indirect call.

Looking at the information about entry points we can see that the
computed stack requirements for the @code{main} entry point is 472
bytes. However, this result is not accurate (as indicated by the mark
@code{+?}), and we see that the two problems that have been found is
the existence of cycles and unbounded frames.

The call chain information provides more detailed information about
the subprograms that are part of a cycle (@code{Q} and @code{R} in
this case), and the dynamic size of the frame for subprogram @code{Q}.

@c *********************************
@node Machine Specific Topics
@chapter Machine Specific Topics

Because stack usage information is only generated for Ada or C code, if you
interface with code written in assembly you may need extra details.

@noindent
@menu
* AVR::
@end menu

@node AVR
@section AVR

The stack used by the @code{call}, @code{rcall}, @code{icall} or @code{eicall}
instructions is counted in the callee. That is all subprograms use at leat
3 bytes on avr6 architecture (@code{atmega256x}) and 2 bytes or avr5
architecture.

If you link with libgcc (which is the default unless you use -nostdlib), add
3 bytes (on avr6 architecture) to the maximum stack usage reported by
@value{gnatstack} as there is some left subprograms written in assembly.

